{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-314513",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the solid, and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\,\\text{feet}, \\\\ y & =3\\,\\text{feet}, \\\\ z & =2\\,\\text{feet}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-1.5\\,\\text{feet}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =1\\,\\text{feet}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-9\\,{\\text{feet}}^{3}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=5\\,\\text{feet},$ $y=3\\,\\text{feet},$ and $z=2\\,\\text{feet}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x\\cdot y\\cdot z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =xy(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})+xz(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+yz(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9 & =(5)(3)(1)+(5)(2)(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+(2)(3)(-1.5) \\\\ -9 & =15+10\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})-9 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the width of the solid is decreasing at a rate of $1.5\\,\\text{feet}/\\text{min}$ at the moment when $x=5\\,\\text{feet},$ $y=2\\,\\text{feet},$ and $z=3\\,\\text{feet}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
